= Getting started
VeraBASIC is a BASIC for Veranda on the Commander X16. This chapter gets a
first program running. VeraBASIC, the editor, writes, checks and runs
programs on the X16 itself (chapter 5. The editor): start it from
/VERANDA/APPS or double-click a .bas file, type the program, press Ctrl+R.
VeraBASIC Run, the runtime alone, runs a program document (and a .bas file
given to it) as it is, and vbasic.py is the tool on the PC that checks
programs, runs them and turns them into program documents; any text editor
writes programs too: on the PC, or Veranda Edit on the X16.

@{What you need}
@{A first program}
@{Running it}
@{Checking and building it on the PC}
@{Running it on the PC}
@{Typing into a program}
@{When something goes wrong}
@{Making a program faster}
@{Making it an application}
@{The parts and where they come from}

= What you need
Where: What
the X16 (or the emulator): Veranda, and VeraBASIC.cvt and VeraBASIC Run.cvt
in /VERANDA/APPS, the help in /VERANDA/HELP
the PC (optional): Python 3 and the Veranda SDK's tools: vbasic.py, with
vbtokens.py, vbtokens_gen.py, vbrom.py, vpack.py and vicon.py beside it (the
tools)
= A first program
Write this into a file called hello.bas:

  ' hello.bas: a first program
  PRINT "Hello from VeraBASIC"
  FOR i% = 1 TO 5
    PRINT i%; "squared is"; i% * i%
  NEXT
  PRINT "Done."

A program is plain text, one statement or more a line. Keywords may be
written in any case. A line may start with a number, as in V2 BASIC, but it
need not. ' starts a comment.

= Running it
Copy hello.bas to the card (or write it there with VeraBASIC or Veranda
Edit) and double-click it in the shell: VeraBASIC, the editor, opens it
(chapter 5); Ctrl+R runs it. The runtime reads the text, tokenizes and
prepares it (a few kilobytes take a fraction of a second; it manages more
than 10 KB of source a second), and runs it. Its window is the console: what
the program prints appears there, line by line, and scrolls when the window
is full. The status bar says whether it is running; Run > Stop, Ctrl+C or
the STOP key stops it. When it ends, the window stays until you press a key,
and the editor comes back.

Names may be long: Bird watch at the feeder.bas opens like hello.bas. The
shell finds VeraBASIC in the folder shown, in /VERANDA/APPS, in /VERANDA or
in the top folder.

If the program has an error that Run finds before it starts (a NEXT without
its FOR, a misspelt number), the status bar and a box show the line and the
message, Line 12: NEXT without FOR for example, and the program does not
run.

= Checking and building it on the PC
On the PC:

python3 vbasic.py check hello.bas python3 vbasic.py build hello.bas -o
hello.cvt

check reports the errors it finds as file:line: message, so a PC editor can
jump to them; Run reports the same messages. build makes the program
document, a GEOS file of class VeraBASIC V1.0 holding the program already
tokenized and prepared. A double click on it starts VeraBASIC Run, which
tokenizes nothing and runs it at once; its name may be long too (-o "Bird
watch at the feeder.cvt"). VeraBASIC Run > Open program... in Run's menu
opens another document; it lists the program documents of the current folder
by their 16-character GEOS names (the shell's double click is the way to
long names and to .bas files).

While a program runs, Veranda goes on working: the menus open, the clock
ticks, and Alt+Tab puts VeraBASIC Run aside with the program in the middle
of what it was doing; brought back from the dock, it goes on from there.
This is because VeraBASIC runs a program in short slices (about 8 ms each)
from the main loop rather than taking over the machine.

= Running it on the PC
vbasic.py run is VeraBASIC's reference interpreter. It runs the same program
and prints the console's lines to the terminal:

python3 vbasic.py run --rom rom.bin hello.bas

Floats in VeraBASIC are the X16 ROM's (the same 5-byte format and maths
library as V2 BASIC), so --rom names an X16 ROM image: the reference then
runs the ROM's own maths routines and its results are those of the X16 to
the last bit. Without a ROM image it uses the PC's floats, which differ in
the last digits. --input FILE gives the keys a program reads with INPUT, a
line of the file to a line typed. The gate of this phase runs every program
of its test corpus both ways and compares the logs.

= Typing into a program
INPUT waits for a line typed in the console, with a ? prompt:

  INPUT "Your name"; name$
  INPUT "Your age"; age%
  PRINT "Hello, "; name$; ". Next year you will be"; age% + 1

  Robin
  7

Backspace takes back a character; Return ends the line. A number that is not
one asks again with Redo from start.

= When something goes wrong
An error that the program does not catch stops it. The console shows the
error's number, its message and the line:

  PRINT "before"
  x = 10 / 0
  PRINT "never printed"

prints Error 11, Division by zero, in line 2. Under VeraBASIC Run a box says
so as well, and the program ends. The next chapter shows how to catch errors
with @TRY ... @CATCH ... @{END TRY}.

= Making a program faster
Two habits make most of the difference:

- Integers. A name ending in % is a 16-bit integer, and arithmetic on
integers never touches the float routines. OPTION INTEGER at the top of a
program makes every name without a suffix an integer too; new programs
should start with it. A FOR loop over an integer counts more than 25,000
times a second.

- Procedures. SUB and FUNCTION with LOCAL variables keep a program readable
at no great cost; GOSUB is there for V2 listings.

= Making it an application
When a program is finished, the editor's File > Make application... turns it
into an application of its own, with its name and its tile in /VERANDA/APPS:
a double click starts it, and nobody needs VeraBASIC to run it. Its @APP
lines give it a name, a tile and the documents it opens; chapter 7, Making
applications, has it all. File > Save program... writes a program document
instead, which VeraBASIC Run opens. On the PC, vbasic.py app and vbasic.py
build do the same.

= The parts and where they come from
Part: What it is
VeraBASIC Run.cvt: the runtime: an interpreter in 6502 assembly, about 24 KB
with its console window, and the tokenizer in 20 KB of overlays
vbasic.py: check, build, app, list, import, run (the reference interpreter),
doc: the tools
docs/vbasic/FORMAT.md: the token format and the program document
The language: the next chapter
The reference: every keyword
The reference card: the same on two pages
See also: @Contents

= The language
This chapter describes VeraBASIC's language: its values, names, expressions,
statements and procedures, without Veranda's extensions (windows, menus,
graphics, files: chapter 3). Every listing here is a whole program; the gate
of phase 1 runs each in VeraBASIC Run and in the reference interpreter and
requires the same output.

@{2.1 Values and names}
@{2.2 Expressions}
@{<> < > <= >=: compare: -1 (true) or 0}
@{2.3 Printing and reading}
@{2.4 Decisions}
@{2.5 Loops}
@{2.6 Jumps and labels}
@{2.7 Procedures}
@{2.8 Arrays}
@{2.9 Strings}
@{2.10 Data}
@{2.11 Errors}
