= 2.1 Values and names
There are three types:

Type: Suffix: Range: Example
integer: %: -32768 to 32767: count%
float: none: about ±1.7E38, 9 digits: price
string: $: 0 to 255 Latin-1 characters: name$
A name is a letter, then letters, digits and _, up to 32 characters, all of
them significant; case does not matter. total, total% and total$ are three
different names. A keyword cannot be a name.

OPTION INTEGER anywhere in a program makes every name without a suffix an
integer; DEFINT A-Z does the same for names starting with the letters given.

  OPTION INTEGER
  a = 7 / 2
  b% = a * 10
  c$ = "seven halves"
  PRINT a; b%; c$

prints 3 30 seven halves: 7 / 2 is 3.5, and assigning it to an integer keeps
the largest whole number not above it.

Numbers are written 12, 1.5, .5, 1E3, 2.5E-3, $FF (hexadecimal) and %1010
(binary). A whole number up to 32767 is an integer; anything else is a
float. Strings are written in quotes, "" for a quote inside one.

= 2.2 Expressions
From the highest priority:

Operators
^: power
-: negation
/ \ MOD: multiply, divide (always a float), integer divide, remainder
+ -: add, subtract; + also joins strings
SHL SHR: shift an integer's bits
= <> < > <= >=: compare: -1 (true) or 0
NOT
AND
OR XOR
AND, OR, XOR and NOT work on the bits of integers, so with comparisons (-1
or 0) they are the logical operators.

Integers stay integers. +, - and of two integers give an integer, computed
without the float routines; when the result does not fit in 16 bits it
becomes a float, so 200 200 is 40000 and not an error. \ divides integers
(rounding towards zero) and MOD gives the remainder (with the sign of the
dividend). / and ^ give floats.

  PRINT 200 * 200; 7 \ 2; -7 \ 2; 7 MOD 3; -7 MOD 3
  PRINT 7 / 2; 2 ^ 10; 2 ^ 0.5
  PRINT 5 AND 3; 5 OR 3; 5 XOR 3; NOT 0
  PRINT 1 < 2; "apple" < "banana"; 3 = 3.0
  PRINT 1 SHL 4; 256 SHR 2

Floats are the X16 ROM's: the 5-byte format of V2 BASIC, about nine digits,
computed by the ROM's maths library, so a float result is what V2 BASIC on
the same machine gives. They print as V2 prints them: a blank or a minus
sign first, then up to nine digits, an exponent for large and small numbers.

  PRINT 1 / 3; 2 / 3; 1E10; 1.5E-5
  PRINT SQR(2); SIN(1); ATN(1) * 4; EXP(1); LOG(10)
  PRINT INT(2.7); INT(-2.7); ABS(-4.5); SGN(-4.5)

RND(1) gives the next random number from 0 up to 1; a negative argument
starts a new sequence from it, so RND(-1) at the start makes a program
repeat its "random" numbers each time.

= 2.3 Printing and reading
PRINT writes values to the console. ; joins them, , moves to the next zone
of 10 columns, and a ; or , at the end keeps the line open. A number prints
with a blank before it (or its minus sign) and one after. TAB(n) moves to
column n, SPC(n) writes n blanks. The console is 78 columns wide; a longer
line goes on in the next row.

  PRINT "Name"; TAB(12); "Count"
  PRINT "Robin"; TAB(12); 3
  PRINT "Wren", "Jay", "Owl"
  PRINT "one";
  PRINT " line"
  PRINT "["; SPC(3); "]"

INPUT "prompt"; a, b$ shows the prompt and ?, and reads values typed on one
line separated by commas (text with a comma in it goes in quotes). Too few
values ask for more with ??; a word where a number belongs asks again with
Redo from start. LINE INPUT "prompt"; s$ reads a whole line as it is. GET k$
gives the next key, or "" if none was typed (it does not wait).

  INPUT "Width, height"; w, h
  PRINT "Area:"; w * h
  LINE INPUT "A note: "; note$
  PRINT "["; note$; "]"

  3.5, 2
  Buy seeds, and nails

= 2.4 Decisions
IF has a one-line form and a block form:

  x% = 12
  IF x% > 10 THEN PRINT "big" ELSE PRINT "small"
  IF x% MOD 2 = 0 THEN PRINT "even": PRINT "and still even"
  IF x% < 0 THEN
    PRINT "negative"
  ELSEIF x% < 10 THEN
    PRINT "one digit"
  ELSE
    PRINT "two digits or more"
  END IF

THEN followed by a line number jumps there, as in V2 BASIC.

SELECT CASE compares one value with lists of values, ranges and comparisons:

  FOR n% = 1 TO 12 STEP 3
    SELECT CASE n%
      CASE 1, 2
        PRINT n%; "small"
      CASE 3 TO 9
        PRINT n%; "middle"
      CASE IS > 9
        PRINT n%; "large"
    END SELECT
  NEXT
  SELECT CASE "wren"
    CASE "robin", "wren": PRINT "a garden bird"
    CASE ELSE: PRINT "something else"
  END SELECT

= 2.5 Loops
  FOR i% = 10 TO 1 STEP -3: PRINT i%;: NEXT: PRINT
  FOR x = 0 TO 1 STEP 0.25: PRINT x;: NEXT: PRINT
  n% = 1
  WHILE n% < 100: n% = n% * 3: WEND
  PRINT n%
  REPEAT: n% = n% - 7: UNTIL n% < 0
  PRINT n%
  DO
    n% = n% + 4
    IF n% > 20 THEN EXIT DO
  LOOP
  PRINT n%
  DO WHILE n% > 10: n% = n% - 5: LOOP
  PRINT n%

A FOR loop whose start is already past its end does not run at all (V2 BASIC
runs it once; the exception is a FOR inside a one-line IF, which keeps V2's
way). EXIT FOR, EXIT WHILE, EXIT DO and EXIT REPEAT leave the innermost loop
of their kind. NEXT j, i ends two loops.

= 2.6 Jumps and labels
A line number or a label is a target for GOTO, GOSUB, RESTORE and RESUME. A
label is a name and a colon at the start of a line.

  GOSUB Header
  FOR k% = 1 TO 3
    ON k% GOSUB 100, 200, 300
  NEXT
  END
  Header: PRINT "-- three subroutines --": RETURN
  100 PRINT "one": RETURN
  200 PRINT "two": RETURN
  300 PRINT "three": RETURN

= 2.7 Procedures
SUB makes a procedure; FUNCTION one that gives a value. Parameters are local
to the procedure and take their arguments by value; BYREF makes a parameter
stand for the caller's variable, and an array parameter (a%()) is always the
caller's array. LOCAL makes more local variables. Procedures may call
themselves.

  SUB Greet(who$, times%)
    LOCAL i%
    FOR i% = 1 TO times%: PRINT "Hello, "; who$: NEXT
  END SUB
  SUB Swap(BYREF a%, BYREF b%)
    LOCAL t%
    t% = a%: a% = b%: b% = t%
  END SUB
  FUNCTION Fact(n)
    IF n <= 1 THEN RETURN 1
    RETURN n * Fact(n - 1)
  END FUNCTION
  FUNCTION Initials$(first$, last$)
    Initials$ = LEFT$(first$, 1) + LEFT$(last$, 1)
  END FUNCTION
  Greet "Robin", 2
  x% = 1: y% = 2: Swap x%, y%: PRINT x%; y%
  PRINT Fact(10); Initials$("Ada", "Lovelace")
  CALL Greet("Wren", 1)

A SUB is called by its name and its arguments, or with CALL. A FUNCTION's
value is set by assigning to its name or by RETURN value. EXIT SUB and EXIT
FUNCTION return at once. A name a procedure does not make local is the
program's (global) variable; SHARED says so on purpose.

Procedure frames, FOR loops and GOSUBs live on the BASIC stack in a RAM bank
of its own, about 8 KB: a recursion several hundred calls deep is fine, a
runaway one ends with error 16, Stack overflow.

= 2.8 Arrays
DIM a%(100) makes an array of 101 elements, indices 0 to 100; up to three
dimensions. Arrays live in RAM banks and may be large: OPTION MEMORY n gives
the variables' arrays and strings up to n banks of 8 KB (4 without it). An
array used without DIM has 11 elements in each dimension, as in V2 BASIC.
UBOUND(a, d) gives a dimension's largest index; REDIM makes an array again
with new sizes.

  DIM squares%(10), grid(2, 3), names$(2)
  FOR i% = 0 TO 10: squares%(i%) = i% * i%: NEXT
  FOR r% = 0 TO 2: FOR c% = 0 TO 3: grid(r%, c%) = r% + c% / 10: NEXT c%, r%
  names$(0) = "Robin": names$(1) = "Wren": names$(2) = "Jay"
  PRINT squares%(10); grid(2, 3); names$(1); UBOUND(grid, 2)
  SUB Total(a%())
    LOCAL i%, s%
    FOR i% = 0 TO UBOUND(a%): s% = s% + a%(i%): NEXT
    PRINT "total"; s%
  END SUB
  Total squares%()

= 2.9 Strings
  s$ = "Blackbird"
  PRINT LEFT$(s$, 5); "|"; RIGHT$(s$, 4); "|"; MID$(s$, 3, 4); "|"; LEN(s$)
  PRINT ASC("A"); CHR$(66); STR$(42); VAL("3.25") * 2
  t$ = s$ + " and " + "Thrush"
  PRINT t$; LEN(t$)
  PRINT "abc" < "abd"; "Zebra" < "apple"

Strings are values: assigning one copies it. They live in a heap in RAM
banks; when the heap is full a collector moves the strings still in use
together in one pass. Each string knows the variable that holds it, so the
collection never searches: a heap of 64 KB is collected in a fraction of a
second, and programs do not stall the way V2 BASIC's did.

= 2.10 Data
DATA lines hold values that READ takes in order; RESTORE starts again, at
the first DATA or at a line given.

  READ n%
  FOR i% = 1 TO n%
    READ bird$, count%
    PRINT bird$; TAB(12); count%
  NEXT
  RESTORE Prices
  READ p1, p2: PRINT p1 + p2
  DATA 3
  DATA Robin, 4, "Blue tit", 7, Wren, 1
  Prices: DATA 2.5, 1.25

CONST name = value names a value that the program cannot change.

= 2.11 Errors
TRY runs statements; an error among them goes to CATCH instead of stopping
the program. ERR is the error's number, ERR$ its message, ERL its line (the
line's number, or its line in the source). ERROR n, message raises an error
of your own; 200 to 255 are free for programs.

  FUNCTION Inverse(x)
    Inverse = 1 / x
  END FUNCTION
  TRY
    PRINT Inverse(4)
    PRINT Inverse(0)
  CATCH
    PRINT "Caught error"; ERR; "("; ERR$; ") in line"; ERL
  END TRY
  TRY
    ERROR 200, "No birds today"
  CATCH
    PRINT ERR; ERR$
  END TRY

An error in a procedure called from inside TRY ends the procedure (and any
between) and goes to the CATCH. V2's ON ERROR GOTO line, RESUME, RESUME NEXT
and ON ERROR GOTO 0 work too. STOP, Ctrl+C and the STOP key raise error 1,
Stopped, which no TRY catches.

= The error numbers
1: Stopped: 13: Type mismatch
2: Syntax error: 14: String too long
3: RETURN without GOSUB: 15: Formula too complex
4: Out of DATA: 16: Stack overflow
5: Illegal quantity: 17: RESUME without error
6: Overflow: 18: NEXT without FOR
7: Out of memory: 19: Wrong number of arguments
8: Undefined line: 20: Not available yet (a Veranda statement of a later
phase)
9: Subscript out of range: 21: Made by a newer VeraBASIC
10: Array already dimensioned: 22: Not inside a SUB
11: Division by zero: 23: BYREF needs a variable
12: Not an array: 200-255: the program's own
= 2.12 V2 listings
A V2 BASIC listing with blanks between its words runs as it is, in the
console:

  10 REM SQUARES AND A STRING BACKWARDS
  20 FOR I=1 TO 5
  30 PRINT I;I*I
  40 NEXT I
  50 A$="COMMODORE":B$=""
  60 FOR I=LEN(A$) TO 1 STEP -1:B$=B$+MID$(A$,I,1):NEXT
  70 PRINT B$
  80 IF I<1 GOTO 100
  90 PRINT "NOT REACHED"
  100 END

What differs: there is no text screen at $0400 (POKE writes main RAM below
$A000, which a program may use from $7F00 with care); LOAD, SAVE and LIST
are the editor's; a FOR whose start is past its end does not run; FRE(0)
gives the free bytes of the variables' banks.

= 2.13 Memory and speed
a program's tokens: up to 64 KB, in RAM banks (7.75 KB of them a bank)
names: a program's names in main RAM, 8 bytes each: about 300
strings and arrays: OPTION MEMORY n banks (4 by default, 24 at most)
the BASIC stack: one bank: procedures, loops, GOSUBs, TRY
floats: the X16 ROM's maths library, called through its jump table
See also: @Contents

= Events
A console program runs from its first line to its last. A Veranda program
does more: it draws its windows and menus, then waits, and the user decides
what happens next by choosing a menu item, clicking, typing, turning the
wheel, closing a window or just letting a timer tick. This chapter is how
VeraBASIC runs such a program; the statements themselves are in Veranda
extensions, part 1.

@{The two halves of a program}
@{The queue}
@{What an event knows}
@{Errors in handlers}
@{Put aside and back}
@{A program's end}

= The two halves of a program
  ' the main lines set things up; the handlers do the work
  OPTION INTEGER
  count = 0
  WINDOW #1, 20, 10, 40, 12, "Counter", CLOSE, STATUS 2
  ON CLOSE #1 CALL Done
  TIMER 1, 2 CALL Tick
  PRINT "the main lines are done"

  SUB Tick
    count = count + 1
    WINDOW STATUS #1, STR$(count), ""
    IF count = 5 THEN Done
  END SUB

  SUB Done
    PRINT "counted"; count
    END
  END SUB

- The main lines run first, top to bottom: open the windows, build the
menus, bind the handlers (@ON ... @CALL, @ITEM, @TIMER), set the variables.

- When they end (or reach WAIT EVENTS), a program that has bound a handler
does not end: it waits for events. A program with no handler ends there, as
a console program does.

- The handlers are SUBs without arguments. Each event calls the one bound to
it. Globals keep their values between handlers; a handler's @LOCAL variables
last for that call only.

- @END, in the main lines or any handler, ends the program. In an
application made from it (chapter 7), and in VeraBASIC Run, a program with
windows that ends quits to the shell; from the editor, the editor comes
back.

= The queue
The kernel never runs BASIC. Its callbacks (a menu item's routine, the key
and press vectors, a process for each @TIMER) only put an event in a queue
of 16 and return; the interpreter takes them out one at a time between
statements:

1. A handler runs to its end before the next starts. Events that come
meanwhile wait in the queue, in their order. 2. A timer has at most one
event waiting: a handler slower than its timer is not flooded, it is called
again as soon as it is done. 3. A full queue drops the newest event (a key
typed into a busy program is lost, as on any machine that is busy). 4. While
a menu is open, or a dialog box is up, BASIC waits: the kernel has the
screen.

The interpreter runs in slices of about 8 ms from the kernel's main loop, so
the pointer, the menus, the clock and the switcher stay alive while BASIC
computes. A long loop in a handler does not stop that, but it does keep the
other handlers waiting; @DOEVENTS inside it runs the ones queued so far, and
@SLEEP takes events while it waits.

  ' a long job that keeps its window's status bar going
  OPTION INTEGER
  WINDOW #1, 20, 10, 40, 12, "Working", STATUS 2
  TIMER 1, 1 CALL ShowTicks
  FOR i = 1 TO 2000
    IF i MOD 250 = 0 THEN DOEVENTS
  NEXT
  PRINT "done after"; ticks; "ticks shown"
  END

  SUB ShowTicks
    ticks = ticks + 1
    WINDOW STATUS #1, "working" + STR$(ticks), ""
  END SUB

= What an event knows
Event: Bound by: In the handler
a menu item: @ITEM text$, sub: @CHECK to tick it; the item's text is not
passed
a click: @ON @CLICK @CALL: @MOUSEX, @MOUSEY (in the window @WINDOW @USE
chose), @MOUSEB (bit 7: the release), @HIT for the part of a window
a key: @ON @KEY @CALL: @KEY$, @KEYCODE, @KEYMODS
the wheel: @ON @WHEEL @CALL: @WHEEL: the turn, -8 to 7
a close glyph: @ON @CLOSE #w @CALL: the window is still open: the handler
closes it, or ends
Quit: @ON @QUIT @CALL: VeraBASIC Run's own Quit (or an application's): the
handler decides; without one, the program ends
a timer: @TIMER n, frames @CALL: @TI, @TIME$
= Errors in handlers
An error in a handler ends the program as an error anywhere does: the editor
shows the line (chapter 6), VeraBASIC Run and an application show a box with
the error and its line, then quit. @TRY ... @CATCH inside the handler keeps
it going:

  OPTION INTEGER
  SUB Safe
    TRY
      x = 10 / zero
    CATCH
      PRINT "caught error"; ERR; "in the handler"
    END TRY
  END SUB
  zero = 0
  Safe
  PRINT "still running"

= Put aside and back
Alt+Tab (or @ASIDE from the program) puts the whole program aside at any
slice, with its windows, its variables and its timers; the dock shows it,
and a click there brings it back where it was: a loop goes on, a timer ticks
again. A program with @APP @STAY is never put aside: the switcher offers to
quit it. @TASKS says how many programs are aside.

= A program's end
At its end (@END, an error, Stop, or Quit) the runtime takes away what the
program set up: its timers' processes, its menus, the key and press vectors,
its sprites, its open files and its RAM banks. The next program, or the
editor, starts clean.

See also: @Contents

= Making applications
A finished program can become a Veranda application of its own: a file in
/VERANDA/APPS with its own name, its own tile in the shell and the dock, the
documents it opens with a double click, and nothing else to install. The
editor's File > Make application... makes it; on the PC, vbasic.py app makes
the same file, byte for byte (apart from the date in the file's directory
entry).

@{What an application is}
@{The APP lines}
@{The tile}
@{Documents}
@{Making it}
@{Bird Watch, the application}
@{Memory}

= What an application is
An application made by VeraBASIC is VeraBASIC Run with the program inside
it. Its file holds Run's own records (the runtime, about 88 KB on the card)
and after them the program's four records, the same ones a program document
holds (docs/vbasic/FORMAT.md, section 7.3). Its info block (the class, the
author, the description, the extensions it opens) and its tile come from the
program's @APP lines. When it starts it finds the program in its own records
and runs it at once: there is no Open program... in its first menu, the menu
is named after the application, and its window is titled with its name.

A .bas file: A program document: An application
made by: you, the editor: File > Save program..., vbasic.py build: File >
Make application..., vbasic.py app
opens with: VeraBASIC (the editor): VeraBASIC Run: itself
needs: VeraBASIC: VeraBASIC Run on the card: nothing
its tile: the shell's for text: VeraBASIC's icon: its own (@APP @TILE)
size: the text: the tokens: the tokens and the runtime, about 90 KB
The application carries the version of the token format it was made with; a
VeraBASIC Run older than the program says Made by a newer VeraBASIC rather
than run it wrongly.

= The APP lines
@APP lines describe the application. They do nothing when the program runs
(from the editor or VeraBASIC Run), so they can stay at the top of the
program while you work on it:

  APP NAME "Bird Watch", CLASS "BirdWatch", VERSION "1.0", AUTHOR "Me"
  APP DESCRIPTION "Counts the birds at the feeder."
  APP DOCUMENTS "BRD"
  APP TILE SKY
  PRINT "The APP lines do nothing when the program runs."

Item: What it gives: Default
NAME n$: the application's name: its file (n$.cvt, up to 63 characters, none
of / : ? = "), its first menu, its window's title, DOCUMENT$'s owner: the
.bas file's name
CLASS c$: its GEOS class (12 characters), which its own GEOS documents name
as their parent: the name's first 12 characters
VERSION v$: the class's version, "1.0" style (6 characters): "1.0"
AUTHOR a$: 19 characters, shown by the shell's Info: none
DESCRIPTION d$: 95 characters, shown by the shell's Info: none
DOCUMENTS e$, ...: up to four file extensions of 1-4 letters or digits: a
double click on such a file starts the application with it (V48): none
TILE colour: the tile's paper: a colour of Look 2 (SKY, ORANGE, DUSK ...):
VeraBASIC Run's (green)
STAY: the switcher never puts it aside: Alt+Tab asks to quit it instead: off
CLASSIC: the first menu is not named after the program: off
Items go on one line or several, separated by commas. The values must be
written out: a string in quotes, a colour by its name or number. The editor
reads the lines from the text itself, so a value worked out by an expression
is an error, APP: NAME "...", CLASS "...", TILE SKY ....

= The tile
The shell shows an application as a tile: a 32 x 32 picture in the ink on a
block of 4 x 4 cards of one paper colour, in a rounded frame the shell
draws. @APP @TILE gives the colour; 32 rows of 32 characters in @TILE DATA
lines give the picture, # for the ink and . for none. Without them the
application has VeraBASIC Run's tile.

  APP TILE ORANGE
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "...............##...............", "..............####..........
  TILE DATA ".............######.............", "............########........
  TILE DATA "...........##########...........", "..........############......
  TILE DATA ".........##############.........", "........################....
  TILE DATA ".......##################.......", "......####################..
  TILE DATA ".....######################.....", "....########################
  TILE DATA "...##########################...", "..##########################
  TILE DATA ".............######.............", ".............######.........
  TILE DATA ".............######.............", ".............######.........
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  TILE DATA "................................", "............................
  PRINT "The tile is the application's, not the program's."

Leave the outermost two pixels empty: the frame is drawn over them. A tile
is easiest to draw in a PC editor with a fixed font; the rows must be
exactly 32 characters, and there must be 32 of them.

= Documents
@APP @DOCUMENTS names the extensions the application opens. A double click
on Garden.brd in the shell then starts Bird Watch with that file; the
program finds its whole name in @DOCUMENT$ and the current folder is the
file's. Started any other way, @DOCUMENT$ is "", and under VeraBASIC Run (or
the editor) it is always "".

  doc$ = DOCUMENT$
  IF doc$ = "" THEN
    PRINT "Started without a document"
  ELSE
    OPEN #1, doc$, "r"
    LINE INPUT #1, a$
    CLOSE #1
    PRINT "The document says "; a$
  END IF

Writing such a file is up to the program: @OPEN, @PRINT # and @CLOSE, in
@FOLDER$(DOCS) for a new one.

= Making it
In the editor, File > Make application...:

1. The program is checked, as Check does (Ctrl+K); an error stops it there,
with the line shown red. 2. The @APP lines are read; an error in them is
shown with its line. 3. The Save dialog opens in /VERANDA/APPS with the
application's name. Choose another folder if you want it elsewhere; a name
without .cvt gets it. An application of that name already there is replaced
after a question. 4. VeraBASIC Run's file is read from /VERANDA/APPS (or the
card's top folder) and the application written: the status bar says Written:
Bird Watch.cvt.

The shell shows it with its tile the next time the folder is shown; a double
click starts it, Alt+Tab puts it aside and the dock brings it back, and its
own Quit (a handler that ends with @END) quits it.

File > Save program... writes a program document instead (class VeraBASIC
V1.0, opened by VeraBASIC Run), as vbasic.py build does.

On the PC:

  python3 -I -B tools/vbasic.py app "Bird Watch.bas" \
      --run "VeraBASIC Run.cvt" -o "Bird Watch.cvt"
  python3 -I -B tools/vbasic.py list "Bird Watch.cvt"

--run names VeraBASIC Run's file (the one on the card, or the SDK's build);
-o defaults to the name from APP NAME. list shows the program inside an
application, as it does a document's.

= Bird Watch, the application
/DOCUMENTS/VeraBASIC Examples/Bird Watch.bas is the tutorial's seventh step
with its @APP lines, a tile of a bird on SKY, .brd documents (a count of
birds, read with @DOCUMENT$ at the start and written by File > Save the
count, into /DOCUMENTS/Sightings.brd when it was started without one) and
File > Put aside (@ASIDE, as Alt+Tab). The package ships it made, as
/VERANDA/APPS/Bird Watch.cvt, with Garden.brd in /DOCUMENTS to open with it.

= Memory
An application needs what VeraBASIC Run needs (13 RAM banks at rest) and one
bank for each 8 KB of its program, so a small one runs in about 15 banks and
can be put aside at 512 KB. Its program has the same room for names in main
RAM as under VeraBASIC Run.

See also: @Contents

= Coming from V2 BASIC
VeraBASIC keeps the core of Commodore BASIC V2, the BASIC of the C64 and of
the X16's ROM: line numbers, @GOTO, @GOSUB, @FOR ... @NEXT, @IF ... @THEN,
@DATA and @READ, @PRINT with ; and ,, the string functions, floats in the
ROM's own format. A V2 listing with blanks between its words runs as it is,
in the console window:

  10 REM A V2 PROGRAM, AS IT IS
  20 DIM N(10)
  30 FOR I=1 TO 10:N(I)=I*I:NEXT I
  40 S=0:FOR I=1 TO 10:S=S+N(I):NEXT
  50 PRINT "SUM OF SQUARES";S
  60 GOSUB 100
  70 END
  100 PRINT "IN THE SUBROUTINE":RETURN

@{Bringing a .PRG in: vbasic.py import}
@{What differs}
@{Speed}

= Bringing a .PRG in: vbasic.py import
A program saved by the C64 or the X16's BASIC is tokenized: its keywords are
single bytes, and a crunched line (FORI=1TO9) has no blanks at all.
vbasic.py import turns such a .PRG into a .bas file:

  python3 -I -B tools/vbasic.py import SQUARES.PRG -o "Squares.bas"

- every keyword token becomes its word with a blank before and after it, so
FORI=1TO9 becomes FOR I=1 TO 9;

- text in quotes, after @REM and in @DATA stays as it was;

- PETSCII becomes Latin-1: the letters as letters (the shifted set as lower
case); a control character in quotes (clear the screen, a colour) becomes "
+ CHR$(147) + ";

- an X16 BASIC statement (the ROM's extended tokens, $CE and a byte: SCREEN,
LINE, RECT ...) has no VeraBASIC form: it is kept as a @REM and named in a
warning, for you to rewrite with the Veranda statements (chapter 3).

Then open it in VeraBASIC (or vbasic.py check it), and Check (Ctrl+K).
Format gives it VeraBASIC's canonical form if you want it.

= What differs
V2: VeraBASIC
names: two significant characters (COUNT and CO are one variable): every
character counts (count and co are two); names are case-blind
keywords inside names (FORM is FOR M): a keyword is a whole word: form is a
name
floats unless % or $: the same; @OPTION @INTEGER makes unsuffixed names
16-bit integers, much faster
a @FOR loop runs once even when its start is past its end: it does not run
@IF ... @THEN on one line: that, and block @IF ... @ELSE ... @{END IF}
@GOSUB and line numbers only: @SUB and @FUNCTION with arguments and @LOCAL
variables, labels, @WHILE, @REPEAT, @DO, @{SELECT CASE}
errors end the program: @TRY ... @CATCH, @ON @ERROR @GOTO and @RESUME
the screen at $0400, @POKE 53280 for the border: no text screen: the console
window; colour with @PAPER; @POKE and @PEEK reach main RAM below $A000
LOAD, SAVE, LIST, RUN: the editor's File and Run menus
@OPEN 1,8,2,"FILE,S,W": @OPEN #1, "A long file name.txt", "w": names up to
63 characters, folders
@GET from the keyboard buffer: @INKEY$, or @ON @KEY @CALL for a windowed
program
@TI and @TI$: @TI (frames), @TIME$, @DATE$; @TIMER for events
@FRE(0): the free bytes of the variables' banks
@SYS: allowed, at your own risk: Veranda's memory map is not the X16 ROM's
= Speed
The same V2 program runs about as fast as on the X16's BASIC (the floats are
the ROM's own routines); with @OPTION @INTEGER at the top and % on the
floats you do not need, loops run three to six times faster (gate vb_speed,
the Rugg/Feldman benchmarks).

  10 OPTION INTEGER
  20 T=0
  30 FOR I=1 TO 1000:T=T+I MOD 7:NEXT
  40 PRINT T

See also: @Contents

= Memory limits
VeraBASIC runs on a Commander X16 with 512 KB of RAM banks or more. A
program's memory is in three places: main RAM for its names, RAM banks for
everything else, and the screen.

@{A program}
@{RAM banks at 512 KB}
@{Main RAM}

= A program
What: Limit: When it is passed
the source text: 64 KB (65,280 bytes): The program is too big
a source line: 255 characters: Line too long
a string: 255 characters: error 14, String too long
the tokens (record 1): 64 KB, in 8 banks of 7.75 KB: Program too big
names: 512, with 7.9 KB of spellings (the X16's tokenizer); in main RAM 8
bytes each at run time, about 290 under VeraBASIC Run, an application or the
editor: Too many names; Too many names for main RAM
procedures: 128, each with 100 parameters and @LOCALs: Program too big
blocks open at once: 24 (@FOR, @IF, @WHILE ... nested in one place): Program
too big
numbered lines: 2,048: Program too big
@DATA items: 4,096: Program too big
arrays and strings: the variables' banks: 4 (32 KB) by default, @OPTION
@MEMORY n up to 24 (192 KB): error 7, Out of memory
the BASIC stack: one bank, 8 KB: about 300 nested procedures, loops, @GOSUBs
and @TRYs: error 16, Stack overflow
an expression: as deep as the value stack holds: error 15, Formula too
complex
windows: 4: error 25, No such window
files open: 3: error 51, Too many files
timers: 8 (@TIMER 1-8): error 5, Illegal quantity
sprites: 2-7 (the kernel keeps the others): error 5, Illegal quantity
file names: 63 characters, folders in them, 64 in all: the kernel's error
= RAM banks at 512 KB
The emulator and a 512 KB X16 have 43 banks for programs once Veranda's
kernel has its own (fewer with fonts loaded or the wallpaper's picture).

Program: Banks at rest: Running a small program
VeraBASIC Run: 13: about 21 (the program's tokens, its stack, its 4 variable
banks)
an application made by VeraBASIC: 13, and one more for each 8 KB of its
program: about 22
VeraBASIC, the editor: 32 (its records, the text's 2, undo's 2): about 40;
the editor's screen is kept in 6 more when 16 are free
The switcher keeps 18 banks free for the desktop, so VeraBASIC Run and a
small application can be put aside at 512 KB; the editor can be at 2 MB, and
at 512 KB its switcher box offers to quit it instead.

= Main RAM
A program's names take 8 bytes each in main RAM, with its procedures'
tables. The room is 2,436 bytes in the editor and 3,052 under VeraBASIC Run
and in an application. @POKE and @PEEK reach main RAM below $A000;
$7F00-$7FFF is safe for a program's own bytes when it has few names.

See also: @Contents

= The ROM maths
VeraBASIC's floats are the Commander X16 ROM's own: the 5-byte format of
Commodore BASIC V2 (an exponent byte and a 4-byte mantissa with its sign),
about nine significant digits, from 2.9E-39 to 1.7E+38. Every float
operation (+ - /, ^, @SQR, @SIN, @LOG, @VAL, @STR$ ...) is a call into the
ROM's maths library through its public jump table, so a VeraBASIC program
computes what the same V2 program computes on the X16, to the last bit.

@{Called, not copied}
@{Two things the runtime does itself}
@{The reference on the PC}
@{The ROM's version}

= Called, not copied
The maths library belongs to the ROM. VeraBASIC carries none of its code or
tables: the runtime calls the jump table's entries (FADD, FMULT, FDIV, FOUT
and the others) at run time, with the ROM bank they are in mapped, and
leaves the library's zero page ($A9-$D3) alone. So the library's licence and
origin stay with the ROM (the plan's section 10).

= Two things the runtime does itself
- Reading a number. The tokenizer turns a float written in the text into its
5 bytes once, when the program is checked. It rounds the spelling correctly
(the PC with Python's fractions, the X16 with big integers in overlay 5),
and does not use the ROM's VAL routine, which differs in the last bit for
some spellings. @VAL at run time is the ROM's.

- Integers. With @OPTION @INTEGER or a % name, values are 16-bit integers
worked out by the runtime: no ROM call, and 3-6 times faster. A float is
made from an integer only where an expression mixes them.

= The reference on the PC
vbasic.py run --rom rom.bin runs the ROM's own maths routines in a small
65C02 emulator (tools/vbrom.py), so the reference interpreter's floats are
the X16's. Without a ROM image it uses the PC's doubles and says the floats
are approximated: the last digit of a result can differ.

  ' the same digits on the X16 and in the reference with --rom
  x = 1 / 3
  PRINT x, x * 3, SQR(2), 10 ^ 0.5
  PRINT INT(-2.5), 7 MOD 3, 2 ^ 31

= The ROM's version
The jump table has kept its entries through the X16's ROM releases. The
gates run under release R49; a ROM whose maths entries moved would be found
by gate vb_core, which compares every float result with the reference's.

See also: @Contents
